{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Y5sLkPAiNmnJk4NJR7yMxN","type":"tool_result","content":"48\t\n49\t// The C code never null-checks `house_t *`, so `house->floors` on an invalid\n50\t// pointer is simply a machine load that faults (SIGSEGV on Linux). A plain Rust\n51\t// `(*house).floors` is compiled with an extra null/alignment UB-check whenever\n52\t// `-C debug-assertions` is on, which would turn that SIGSEGV into a SIGABRT and\n53\t// therefore diverge from C. Going through `addr_of!` + `read_volatile` /\n54\t// `write_volatile` emits exactly the same single load/store the C compiler emits\n55\t// and preserves C's fault behaviour in every build profile. For all valid\n56\t// pointers the observable result is bit-for-bit identical.\n57\t#[inline]\n58\tunsafe fn get_floors(house: *mut house_t) -> c_int {\n59\t    read_volatile(addr_of!((*house).floors))\n60\t}\n61\t#[inline]\n62\tunsafe fn set_floors(house: *mut house_t, v: c_int) {\n63\t    write_volatile(addr_of_mut!((*house).floors), v);\n64\t}\n65\t#[inline]\n66\tunsafe fn get_bedrooms(house: *mut house_t) -> c_int {\n67\t    read_volatile(addr_of!((*house).bedrooms))\n68\t}\n69\t#[inline]\n70\tunsafe fn set_bedrooms(house: *mut house_t, v: c_int) {\n71\t    write_volatile(addr_of_mut!((*house).bedrooms), v);\n72\t}\n73\t#[inline]\n74\tunsafe fn get_bathrooms(house: *mut house_t) -> c_double {\n75\t    read_volatile(addr_of!((*house).bathrooms))\n76\t}\n77\t#[inline]\n78\tunsafe fn set_bathrooms(house: *mut house_t, v: c_double) {\n79\t    write_volatile(addr_of_mut!((*house).bathrooms), v);\n80\t}\n81\t\n82\t// C: static void add_floor(house_t *house) { house->floors++; }\n83\t//\n84\t// `house->floors++` on `INT_MAX` is signed overflow (UB in C); gcc at the\n85\t// optimisation level used by c_src/CMakeLists.txt wraps two's-complement, so\n86\t// `wrapping_add` reproduces it.\n87\tunsafe fn add_floor(house: *mut house_t) {"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_